# Return to the Common Chord

## Scene summary (from gm-assist, verbatim)

#### The party arrives at their former haunt to find it has become the most popular venue in Neverwinter, now boasting a six-week reservation list.

- The Common Chord was once the sort of venue that asked few questions and paid in actual coin.
- Its main room held approximately forty people comfortably. During Vukradin's original three-night engagement, the average audience was closer to eleven.
- Petra, the half-elf proprietor, initially paid Vukradin, but he returned the money offered by anyone who did not rate his performance ten out of ten. He retained Brewbarry's payment because Brewbarry was an enthusiastic fan.
- Petra had told Vukradin that she enjoyed his music but did not book him again, helping set him on the path toward adventuring.
- The Common Chord now has a six-week reservation list and has become one of Neverwinter's fashionable destinations.
- Although most patrons do not initially recognize Vukradin himself, Brewbarry's appearance and Valphine's white dragon plate armor immediately attract attention.
- Several patrons dressed as Brewbarry-style barbarians begin chanting.
- Brewbarry tries to arrange merchandise pre-orders despite having no stock beyond the bathrobe he is wearing.
- Vukradin announces, “We're Lathander's Death! We're here to perform!” His successful persuasion check turns uncertainty into cheering and screaming.
- Petra arrives amid the commotion and proudly declares, “I discovered this guy.”
- Vukradin confronts Petra about the sign advertising a performance he never agreed to.
- Petra reveals that the advertised appearance was intended for a cover band that plays Vukradin's old songs every night.
- She insists she has always disclosed that the performer was not the real Vukradin and proclaims her respect for “the one, the only, the great Vukradin.”
- Petra twice refuses a 500 gold piece fee outright: “I can't afford 500 gold pieces for a one-night performance of the Lathander's Death.”
- Brewbarry floats a revenue share instead — fifty percent of the house on the night of the concert — and the party holds out for gross rather than net; Petra takes it, shaking on the deal before anyone can change their mind.
- Vukradin accuses Petra of profiting from his image and reputation and argues that five hundred gold is a fair settlement for prior use.
- With Brewbarry looming, Vukradin settles the debt without immediate coin: one exclusive performance in two nights and a perpetual licence allowing Petra to continue using his image, history, and cover band, provided she keeps identifying the act as a cover band.
- Petra reminds Vukradin that she gave him a stage when nobody else would: “I was there at the beginning. I was the one who gave you the gig.”
- The band performs a one-song teaser. Vukradin rolls poorly, and Valphine produces a natural one.
- Valphine's lack of harmony and off-cue screeches accidentally reproduce Vukradin's early discordant style, creating an authentically avant-garde performance that pleases the crowd.
- Petra preserves a polished table surrounded by fine couches and bearing a plaque stating that Vukradin created his music there. In reality, it is where Brewbarry used to sit.
- When Petra asks Vukradin to sign the plaque, he answers, “Well, that'll be extra.”
- Vukradin negotiates a twenty-five-gold-piece fee for the autograph, which Petra pays immediately.
- Petra asks Brewbarry, “What did you do to this guy? This guy used to be the… the nicest guy!”
- Brewbarry proposes using the band's popularity to influence public opinion against Lord Neverember, but the group remembers that Neverember may be an ally against House Margaster and changes targets.
- Brewbarry states the goal plainly: “Every people… leaves this tavern tonight needs to think that House Margaster is the most evil organization.”
- The second performance is strengthened by Bardic Inspiration and further magical aid.
- The song contains witty lyrics about House Margaster's trade deals, its grip on supply routes, and the inflationary suffering imposed on ordinary people.
- The crowd begins humming and repeating the tune, allowing the anti-Margaster message to spread.
- The Common Chord now serves elaborate food, unlike the days when it offered no meals and only cheap liquor.
- Its beer has also improved considerably. Brewbarry, who once considered the old swill excellent, claims that anything capable of making him drunk is good.
- Petra consequently reserves the cheap beer for Brewbarry and the better drink for party members capable of appreciating it.
- The party plans to leave after the performance to conduct the third-hour stakeout, with a possible sewer expedition to follow.

